description:
Site: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/space.rs:976-990 (handle_space_set update branch — patch-key bucketing).

Current code:

    for (key, value) in std::mem::take(&mut patch_map) {
        if let Some(&canonical) = STRUCTURAL_KEYS.iter().find(|&&k| k == key) {
            ...
        } else if METADATA_FIELDS.contains(&key.as_str()) {
            ...
        } else {
            unknown_keys.push(key);
        }
    }

Two related issues:

1. STRUCTURAL_KEYS.iter().find(|&&k| k == key) is a linear-scan-by-equality through a 12-element slice when the natural Rust idiom is `match key.as_str() { "addRoles" | "removeRoles" | ... => ..., }`. The `.find()` form returns Option<&&str>, which is awkward (double-ref); the match form returns nothing extra. The find()-with-predicate is exactly the shape clippy's `manual_find` lint flags in newer versions.

2. The same iterator runs membership-tests against both STRUCTURAL_KEYS and METADATA_FIELDS — 12 + 5 = 17 string-equality comparisons per patch key in the worst case. A single match over the union of all valid keys would be O(1) per key (table-dispatch).

What goes wrong if left: not a correctness bug — both forms produce identical behavior — but the find()-on-slice-by-equality is a code-review tell that the author defaulted to method chains instead of language constructs. The match form is faster (the optimizer can produce a jump table), more idiomatic, and shorter. Combined with finding #29 (the same STRUCTURAL_KEYS slice + canonical str dispatch), this is the same anti-pattern in two places.

Idiomatic rewrite:

    enum PatchKind { Structural(StructuralKey), Metadata(MetadataField), Unknown }
    fn classify(key: &str) -> PatchKind { match key { ... } }

    for (key, value) in std::mem::take(&mut patch_map) {
        match classify(&key) {
            PatchKind::Structural(canon) => match parse_structural_entries(canon, value) { ... },
            PatchKind::Metadata(field) => { clean_patch.insert(key, value); }
            PatchKind::Unknown => unknown_keys.push(key),
        }
    }

Combined with #29, both findings dissolve into a single enum-based dispatch.

Scope: P2 type-design. Pairs with #29; together they argue for one consolidating refactor rather than two independent ones.
